Examples: I still remember the afternoon *when* we lost electricity. 2020 was the year *in which* the new system was introduced.
Watch out: If the time is the object of the verb, use *which* or *that*, not *when*: *I enjoyed the holiday that we spent together*, not *the holiday when we spent together*.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To add information about a time. *When* is common in everyday English; *in which*, *at which* or *on which* is more formal."},{"label":"Key difference","text":"*in which* = years, months and longer periods; *at which* = exact times and moments; *on which* = days and dates."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a time noun before the gap (*the day*, *the year*, *the moment*, *the date*)."},{"label":"Examples","text":"I still remember the afternoon *when* we lost electricity. 2020 was the year *in which* the new system was introduced."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"If the time is the object of the verb, use *which* or *that*, not *when*: *I enjoyed the holiday that we spent together*, not *the holiday when we spent together*."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-03-013-06-00';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Where to put the preposition (*to whom* … / *who* … *to*)', body = 'When to use: When the verb in the clause needs a preposition (*talk to*, *rely on*, *apply for*, *stay at*).
Key difference: Formal: put the preposition at the start, followed by *whom* or *which* (*the landlord to whom I wrote*). Everyday: put the preposition at the end, with *who*, *that*, *which* or no pronoun (*the landlord I wrote to*).
Look for: verbs that go with a particular preposition, and whether the preposition is already at the start or the end.
Examples: The landlord *to whom* I sent the payment replied quickly. The landlord (*who*) I sent the payment *to* replied quickly.
Watch out: Use the preposition only once: *the manager to whom I spoke* or *the manager who I spoke to*, not *the manager to whom I spoke to*. Do not use *who* or *that* straight after a preposition.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"When the verb in the clause needs a preposition (*talk to*, *rely on*, *apply for*, *stay at*)."},{"label":"Key difference","text":"Formal: put the preposition at the start, followed by *whom* or *which* (*the landlord to whom I wrote*). Everyday: put the preposition at the end, with *who*, *that*, *which* or no pronoun (*the landlord I wrote to*)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"verbs that go with a particular preposition, and whether the preposition is already at the start or the end."},{"label":"Examples","text":"The landlord *to whom* I sent the payment replied quickly. The landlord (*who*) I sent the payment *to* replied quickly."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Use the preposition only once: *the manager to whom I spoke* or *the manager who I spoke to*, not *the manager to whom I spoke to*. Do not use *who* or *that* straight after a preposition."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-03-013-07-00';
