Look for: *while*, *after*, *before*, *on* or *upon* followed by an *-ing* form.
Example: *Upon arriving* at the hotel, we checked in immediately.
Watch out: The person after the comma must be the one doing the action: *While waiting for the bus, I saw a friend*, not *While waiting for the bus, a friend saw me*.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To shorten a time clause when both parts have the same subject (*While I was waiting* → *While waiting*)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"*while*, *after*, *before*, *on* or *upon* followed by an *-ing* form."},{"label":"Example","text":"*Upon arriving* at the hotel, we checked in immediately."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"The person after the comma must be the one doing the action: *While waiting for the bus, I saw a friend*, not *While waiting for the bus, a friend saw me*."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-05-025-23-00';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Linking words *yet* and *so*', body = 'When to use: To join two complete sentences. *yet* = but surprisingly; *so* = therefore.
Look for: a comma between two full clauses.
Example: He studied hard, *yet* he failed the test.
Watch out: Do not use *yet* or *so* together with *although* or *because* in the same sentence.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To join two complete sentences. *yet* = but surprisingly; *so* = therefore."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a comma between two full clauses."},{"label":"Example","text":"He studied hard, *yet* he failed the test."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Do not use *yet* or *so* together with *although* or *because* in the same sentence."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-05-025-24-00';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Past participle openers (*Startled by…*, *Designed by…*)', body = 'When to use: To start a sentence with a passive idea about the subject (*Startled by the noise* = because she was startled).
Look for: a sentence opener that describes what happened to the subject.
Example: *Startled* by the noise, the cat jumped off the sofa.
Watch out: The subject after the comma must be the one affected: *Designed by top engineers, the bridge survived the storm*, not *Designed by top engineers, the storm hit the bridge*.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To start a sentence with a passive idea about the subject (*Startled by the noise* = because she was startled)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a sentence opener that describes what happened to the subject."},{"label":"Example","text":"*Startled* by the noise, the cat jumped off the sofa."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"The subject after the comma must be the one affected: *Designed by top engineers, the bridge survived the storm*, not *Designed by top engineers, the storm hit the bridge*."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-05-025-25-00';
