Look for: *if* + present, then *will* / *won''t* + base verb.
Example: *If* it *rains* tomorrow, we *will cancel* the picnic.
Watch out: Do not put *will* in the *if*-part: *If it rains tomorrow*, not *If it will rain tomorrow*.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"For a situation that may really happen in the future and its likely result."},{"label":"Look for","text":"*if* + present, then *will* / *won''t* + base verb."},{"label":"Example","text":"*If* it *rains* tomorrow, we *will cancel* the picnic."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Do not put *will* in the *if*-part: *If it rains tomorrow*, not *If it will rain tomorrow*."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-06-026-18-00';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Zero conditional: facts and general truths', body = 'When to use: For things that are always true, such as scientific facts, rules and habits.
Look for: *if* / *when* + present, then present in the main clause.
Example: *If* you *heat* ice, it *melts*.
Watch out: For general facts, both parts usually stay in the present: *If you heat ice, it melts* is the standard form.', fields_json = '[{"label":"When to use","text":"For things that are always true, such as scientific facts, rules and habits."},{"label":"Look for","text":"*if* / *when* + present, then present in the main clause."},{"label":"Example","text":"*If* you *heat* ice, it *melts*."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"For general facts, both parts usually stay in the present: *If you heat ice, it melts* is the standard form."}]' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-06-026-19-00';
