UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Tenses & Time Expressions', body = 'You have a good basic understanding of English tenses, but you may find it harder when a sentence connects several different times. Pay attention to the differences between actions that are finished, still continuing, or completed before another time, for example *had been waiting* and *will have finished*.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-01-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Tenses & Time Expressions', body = 'You may find tenses challenging at the moment, especially when deciding how actions in the past, present, and future are connected. Start by reviewing the main tenses and the differences between *I did*, *I have done*, and *I had done*. Regular practice will help you build confidence.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-01-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Modal Verbs: Certainty, Possibility & Advice', body = 'You use clues in a sentence very effectively to understand certainty, possibility, advice, and criticism. You can distinguish meanings expressed by modal verbs such as *must*, *can''t*, *might*, *could*, *should*, and *should have*. This helps you understand both what a speaker thinks and how strongly they believe it.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-02-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Modal Verbs: Certainty, Possibility & Advice', body = 'You have a strong understanding of modal verbs and their meanings. You can usually recognise whether a speaker is making a likely conclusion, expressing possibility, giving advice, or commenting on a past action. Practise the more subtle differences between forms such as *might have*, *could have*, and *should have*.', fields_json = '{"band":"strong","minScore":4,"maxScore":4}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-02-000-00-02';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Modal Verbs: Certainty, Possibility & Advice', body = 'You understand the basic meanings of modal verbs such as *can*, *must*, and *should*. However, you may find it harder to tell how certain a speaker is, or to understand comments about the past such as *might have*, *must have*, and *should have*. Practise noticing the clues in a sentence that show how sure the speaker is.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-02-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Modal Verbs: Certainty, Possibility & Advice', body = 'You may find modal verbs challenging at the moment. Focus first on how words such as *must*, *might*, *can''t*, and *should* express certainty, possibility, and advice. Then move on to past forms such as *must have* and *could have*.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-02-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Adjective (Relative) Clauses', body = 'You show excellent understanding of relative clauses, which add information about people, things, places, or whole ideas. You can choose suitable words such as *who*, *whose*, *which*, *that*, and *whom*, and you understand when commas are needed. You can also recognise shorter forms of relative clauses used in more formal writing.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-03-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Adjective (Relative) Clauses', body = 'You understand simple relative clauses, but more complex sentences may still cause difficulty. Practise choosing between *who*, *whom*, *whose*, *which*, and *that*, deciding when commas are needed, and recognising the shorter forms used in formal writing.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-03-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Adjective (Relative) Clauses', body = 'You may find it difficult at the moment to add information about a noun using a relative clause. Start by reviewing when to use *who*, *which*, *that*, and *whose*, and the difference between information that is essential and information that is extra.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-03-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Noun Clauses & Formal Academic Expressions', body = 'You recognise many common clause patterns, but you may find more formal structures harder. Pay attention to word order in indirect questions (*I wonder where he is*, not *I wonder where is he*), tense changes in reported information, and the noun phrases used in academic writing.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-04-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Noun Clauses & Formal Academic Expressions', body = 'You may find noun clauses and formal academic expressions challenging at the moment. Start by reviewing how indirect questions are formed and how words such as *that*, *whether*, and *what* introduce a clause within a longer sentence.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-04-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Linking Ideas & Sentence Relationships', body = 'You show excellent understanding of how English links ideas. You can recognise language used to express time, reason, result, contrast, condition, and concession, for example *although*, *unless*, *therefore*, and *nevertheless*. This skill helps you follow and produce clear, well-organised arguments.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-05-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Linking Ideas & Sentence Relationships', body = 'You can identify common linking words, but you may find it harder to choose between words with similar meanings. Practise the differences between expressions of contrast, concession, and cause and effect, such as *although*, *whereas*, *however*, and *therefore*.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-05-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Linking Ideas & Sentence Relationships', body = 'You may find it challenging at the moment to choose the right words to link ideas. Start by reviewing common linking words for time, reason, result, contrast, and condition, such as *when*, *because*, *so*, *but*, and *if*, and then move on to more formal expressions.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-05-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Conditionals & Unreal Situations', body = 'You show excellent understanding of conditional sentences and hypothetical language. You can distinguish between real possibilities, imagined present or future situations, and unreal past situations. You also understand expressions such as *wish*, *if only*, and formal patterns such as *Had I known…*.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-06-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Conditionals & Unreal Situations', body = 'You understand common conditional sentences, but you may find more complex patterns harder. Practise sentences that mix past and present time (*If I had studied harder, I would be at university now*), formal patterns such as *Had I known…*, and expressions such as *I wish* and *if only*.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-06-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Conditionals & Unreal Situations', body = 'You may find conditional sentences challenging at the moment. Start by reviewing the main patterns for real possibilities, imagined situations, and regrets about the past, paying close attention to which tenses go together. Then practise *wish* and *if only*.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-06-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Reported Speech', body = 'You show excellent understanding of how direct speech changes when it is reported. You can recognise changes in tense, pronouns, time words, and place words, and you understand how to report statements, questions, instructions, and requests. You also recognise the different patterns used after reporting verbs such as *tell*, *ask*, *insist*, and *deny*.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-07-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Reported Speech', body = 'You can report simple statements well, but more complex reported speech may still cause difficulty. Pay attention to changes in time and place words (*tomorrow* → *the next day*), word order in reported questions, and the patterns that follow verbs such as *insist*, *deny*, and *accuse*.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-07-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Reported Speech', body = 'You may find reported speech challenging at the moment. Start by reviewing how tenses, pronouns, and time and place words change when someone’s words are reported, and how questions are reported (*She asked where I lived*).', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-07-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Gerunds & Infinitives', body = 'You show excellent understanding of verb patterns involving *-ing* forms and *to + verb* forms. You can recognise which form follows particular verbs, adjectives, and prepositions, and you understand that changing the form can sometimes change the meaning. This is an important skill for producing natural and accurate English.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-08-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Gerunds & Infinitives', body = 'You have a strong understanding of when to use an *-ing* form or an infinitive. You can usually choose the correct pattern after common verbs, adjectives, and prepositions. Continue practising verbs whose meaning changes depending on the form that follows, such as *remember*, *stop*, and *try*.', fields_json = '{"band":"strong","minScore":4,"maxScore":4}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-08-000-00-02';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Gerunds & Infinitives', body = 'You know many common verb patterns, but you may find it harder when the choice between *-ing* and *to + verb* is less predictable. Practise verbs whose meaning changes depending on the form that follows (*stop*, *remember*, *try*) and phrases where *to* is followed by *-ing* (*look forward to seeing*).', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-08-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Gerunds & Infinitives', body = 'You may find it challenging at the moment to decide whether a word is followed by *-ing* or *to + verb*. Start by learning the patterns that follow common verbs, adjectives, and prepositions, and keep a list of examples as you read.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-08-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Formal Grammar & Official Language', body = 'You have a general sense of formal English, but you may not yet recognise its grammar consistently. Practise the structures used for recommendations and requests, such as *They recommended that he apply*, and the fixed expressions used in official writing.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-09-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Formal Grammar & Official Language', body = 'You may be less familiar with the grammar used in formal and official English. Start by reviewing how recommendations and requests are expressed after words such as *suggest*, *recommend*, *insist*, and *it is essential that*, where the basic form of the verb is often used.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-09-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Inversion & Emphasis', body = 'You understand normal word order well, but you may find it harder when the order changes for emphasis. Practise sentences that begin with words such as *Never*, *Rarely*, or *Not only*, patterns such as *No sooner had… than…*, and descriptive sentences such as *Into the room walked…*.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-10-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Inversion & Emphasis', body = 'You may find it challenging at the moment to recognise sentences in which the usual word order changes for emphasis. Start by reviewing sentences that begin with negative words such as *Never*, *Seldom*, and *Rarely*, and notice how the verb comes before the subject (*Never have I seen…*).', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-10-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Participial Phrases & Reduced Clauses', body = 'You show excellent understanding of shorter clause forms that use *-ing*, *-ed*, or *having + past participle*. You can recognise how these phrases add information about time, reason, action, or description. You also understand that the phrase must clearly refer to the correct person or thing in the main clause.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-11-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Participial Phrases & Reduced Clauses', body = 'You have a strong understanding of participial phrases and reduced clauses. You can usually recognise how writers make sentences shorter and more varied by replacing full clauses with *-ing*, *-ed*, or perfect participle phrases. Continue checking carefully that the phrase clearly describes the correct subject.', fields_json = '{"band":"strong","minScore":4,"maxScore":4}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-11-000-00-02';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Participial Phrases & Reduced Clauses', body = 'You understand some shorter clause forms, but more complex ones may still cause difficulty. Practise phrases with *having + past participle* (*Having finished…*) and passive forms (*Having been told…*), and check carefully that each phrase refers to the correct subject.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-11-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Participial Phrases & Reduced Clauses', body = 'You may find it challenging at the moment to understand how longer clauses can be shortened into *-ing* or *-ed* phrases. Start by reviewing simple examples (*Feeling tired, she went home*) and check that the phrase describes the person or thing that follows it.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-11-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Prepositions & Common Word Combinations', body = 'You show excellent understanding of prepositions and common word combinations in English. You can recognise which prepositions naturally follow particular verbs, adjectives, and nouns, such as *insist on*, *capable of*, and *insight into*. You also understand longer phrases such as *in accordance with* and *with a view to*.', fields_json = '{"band":"excellent","minScore":5,"maxScore":5}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-12-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Prepositions & Common Word Combinations', body = 'You have a good general vocabulary, but you may sometimes choose the wrong preposition in fixed combinations. Pay attention to the prepositions that follow particular verbs, adjectives, and nouns, such as *insist on*, *capable of*, and *insight into*.', fields_json = '{"band":"approaching","minScore":2,"maxScore":3}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-12-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Prepositions & Common Word Combinations', body = 'You may find it challenging at the moment to choose the correct preposition after particular words. Start by learning common combinations such as *interested in*, *depend on*, and *responsible for*, and keep a list of new examples as you read.', fields_json = '{"band":"beginning","minScore":0,"maxScore":1}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-12-000-00-04';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'No priority areas', body = 'You have shown strong control across all 12 grammar areas. To keep improving, focus on using a wide range of structures in your own writing and speaking, especially more formal patterns such as inversion and *that*-clauses with the base verb. Reading demanding texts regularly will help you keep this level.', fields_json = '{"bucket":"none","minPriority":0,"maxPriority":0,"intro":"You have shown strong control across all 12 grammar areas. To keep improving, focus on using a wide range of structures in your own writing and speaking, especially more formal patterns such as inversion and *that*-clauses with the base verb. Reading demanding texts regularly will help you keep this level.","steps":[],"closing":""}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-00-000-00-00';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = '1–2 priority areas', body = 'Your grammar is strong overall, with just one or two areas to work on. Focusing on these for a few weeks should make a clear difference, and the rest of your grammar gives you a solid base to build on. For each of the areas marked as priorities:
Review: read the explanations and examples in this report, and check the grammar point in a good grammar reference (for example, *English Grammar in Use* or *Advanced Grammar in Use*).
Notice: look out for these structures when you read articles, reports and academic texts. Note them down, or highlight them and ask yourself why the writer chose that form.
Practise: complete a few short exercises on the same point, then write two or three sentences of your own using it.
Use: try to include the structure in your next essay, email or presentation. Using a structure yourself is the best way to make it stick.
Check: when you proofread your work, look for the mistakes listed under ‘Watch out’ for each grammar point.
Small, regular practice (15–20 minutes a few times a week) works better than long, occasional study sessions. Keep using the areas you are strong in too, so that they stay accurate and become a natural part of your writing and speaking.', fields_json = '{"bucket":"oneToTwo","minPriority":1,"maxPriority":2,"intro":"Your grammar is strong overall, with just one or two areas to work on. Focusing on these for a few weeks should make a clear difference, and the rest of your grammar gives you a solid base to build on. For each of the areas marked as priorities:","steps":[{"label":"Review","text":"read the explanations and examples in this report, and check the grammar point in a good grammar reference (for example, *English Grammar in Use* or *Advanced Grammar in Use*)."},{"label":"Notice","text":"look out for these structures when you read articles, reports and academic texts. Note them down, or highlight them and ask yourself why the writer chose that form."},{"label":"Practise","text":"complete a few short exercises on the same point, then write two or three sentences of your own using it."},{"label":"Use","text":"try to include the structure in your next essay, email or presentation. Using a structure yourself is the best way to make it stick."},{"label":"Check","text":"when you proofread your work, look for the mistakes listed under ‘Watch out’ for each grammar point."}],"closing":"Small, regular practice (15–20 minutes a few times a week) works better than long, occasional study sessions. Keep using the areas you are strong in too, so that they stay accurate and become a natural part of your writing and speaking."}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-00-000-00-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = '3–4 priority areas', body = 'You have a good base in most areas, with a few that need more attention. Work through the priority areas one at a time, starting with the one where you scored lowest, and come back to the explanations in this report as you practise. For each of the areas marked as priorities:
Review: read the explanations and examples in this report, and check the grammar point in a good grammar reference (for example, *English Grammar in Use* or *Advanced Grammar in Use*).
Notice: look out for these structures when you read articles, reports and academic texts. Note them down, or highlight them and ask yourself why the writer chose that form.
Practise: complete a few short exercises on the same point, then write two or three sentences of your own using it.
Use: try to include the structure in your next essay, email or presentation. Using a structure yourself is the best way to make it stick.
Check: when you proofread your work, look for the mistakes listed under ‘Watch out’ for each grammar point.
Small, regular practice (15–20 minutes a few times a week) works better than long, occasional study sessions. Keep using the areas you are strong in too, so that they stay accurate and become a natural part of your writing and speaking.', fields_json = '{"bucket":"threeToFour","minPriority":3,"maxPriority":4,"intro":"You have a good base in most areas, with a few that need more attention. Work through the priority areas one at a time, starting with the one where you scored lowest, and come back to the explanations in this report as you practise. For each of the areas marked as priorities:","steps":[{"label":"Review","text":"read the explanations and examples in this report, and check the grammar point in a good grammar reference (for example, *English Grammar in Use* or *Advanced Grammar in Use*)."},{"label":"Notice","text":"look out for these structures when you read articles, reports and academic texts. Note them down, or highlight them and ask yourself why the writer chose that form."},{"label":"Practise","text":"complete a few short exercises on the same point, then write two or three sentences of your own using it."},{"label":"Use","text":"try to include the structure in your next essay, email or presentation. Using a structure yourself is the best way to make it stick."},{"label":"Check","text":"when you proofread your work, look for the mistakes listed under ‘Watch out’ for each grammar point."}],"closing":"Small, regular practice (15–20 minutes a few times a week) works better than long, occasional study sessions. Keep using the areas you are strong in too, so that they stay accurate and become a natural part of your writing and speaking."}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-00-000-00-02';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = '5 or more priority areas', body = 'Several areas need more practice, and this is common at this stage. Choose one area to start with, ideally one you use often in your studies, and make steady progress before moving on to the next. For each of the areas marked as priorities:
Review: read the explanations and examples in this report, and check the grammar point in a good grammar reference (for example, *English Grammar in Use* or *Advanced Grammar in Use*).
Notice: look out for these structures when you read articles, reports and academic texts. Note them down, or highlight them and ask yourself why the writer chose that form.
Practise: complete a few short exercises on the same point, then write two or three sentences of your own using it.
Use: try to include the structure in your next essay, email or presentation. Using a structure yourself is the best way to make it stick.
Check: when you proofread your work, look for the mistakes listed under ‘Watch out’ for each grammar point.
Small, regular practice (15–20 minutes a few times a week) works better than long, occasional study sessions. Keep using any areas you are already confident in too, so that they stay accurate and become a natural part of your writing and speaking.', fields_json = '{"bucket":"fiveOrMore","minPriority":5,"maxPriority":12,"intro":"Several areas need more practice, and this is common at this stage. Choose one area to start with, ideally one you use often in your studies, and make steady progress before moving on to the next. For each of the areas marked as priorities:","steps":[{"label":"Review","text":"read the explanations and examples in this report, and check the grammar point in a good grammar reference (for example, *English Grammar in Use* or *Advanced Grammar in Use*)."},{"label":"Notice","text":"look out for these structures when you read articles, reports and academic texts. Note them down, or highlight them and ask yourself why the writer chose that form."},{"label":"Practise","text":"complete a few short exercises on the same point, then write two or three sentences of your own using it."},{"label":"Use","text":"try to include the structure in your next essay, email or presentation. Using a structure yourself is the best way to make it stick."},{"label":"Check","text":"when you proofread your work, look for the mistakes listed under ‘Watch out’ for each grammar point."}],"closing":"Small, regular practice (15–20 minutes a few times a week) works better than long, occasional study sessions. Keep using any areas you are already confident in too, so that they stay accurate and become a natural part of your writing and speaking."}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-01-00-000-00-03';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'UNEVEN RESULTS', body = 'Interestingly, you answered some higher-level questions correctly but missed some easier ones. This may suggest gaps in common vocabulary, or that some answers were good guesses. Reviewing everyday vocabulary will help make your knowledge more secure.', fields_json = '{"key":"uneven","condition":"*When higher-level answers meet the threshold but lower-level answers missed,*"}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-02-00-000-01-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'CLOSE TO THE NEXT BAND', body = 'You are close to the next level, so with steady practice you could reach it soon.', fields_json = '{"key":"closeToNext","condition":"*When the test taker is one point less than the threshold,*"}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-02-00-000-01-02';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Foundation', body = 'Learn the most common 2,000–3,000 words of English (for example, [the Oxford 3000](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/wordlist/american_english/oxford3000/)), a few each day.
Read graded readers at your level for 15–20 minutes a day. Choose books where you know most of the words.
Keep a vocabulary notebook or app. Note each word with an example sentence, not only a translation.
Use a flashcard app with spaced review (for example, Quizlet or Anki) so you see words again before you forget them.
Learn word families together (*decide*, *decision*, *decisive*).', fields_json = '{"level":"A2","bullets":["Learn the most common 2,000–3,000 words of English (for example, [the Oxford 3000](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/wordlist/american_english/oxford3000/)), a few each day.","Read graded readers at your level for 15–20 minutes a day. Choose books where you know most of the words.","Keep a vocabulary notebook or app. Note each word with an example sentence, not only a translation.","Use a flashcard app with spaced review (for example, Quizlet or Anki) so you see words again before you forget them.","Learn word families together (*decide*, *decision*, *decisive*)."]}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-02-00-000-02-00';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Intermediate', body = 'Move on to [the Oxford 5000](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/wordlists/oxford3000-5000) or the B2 words in the English Vocabulary Profile.
Read short news articles and listen to podcasts on topics you enjoy, such as BBC Learning English.
Learn words in common combinations, not one at a time (*make a decision*, *heavy traffic*).
Use a learner''s dictionary to check the different meanings of a word and how it is used in sentences.
Try to use two or three new words each week in your own writing or speaking.', fields_json = '{"level":"B1","bullets":["Move on to [the Oxford 5000](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/wordlists/oxford3000-5000) or the B2 words in the English Vocabulary Profile.","Read short news articles and listen to podcasts on topics you enjoy, such as BBC Learning English.","Learn words in common combinations, not one at a time (*make a decision*, *heavy traffic*).","Use a learner''s dictionary to check the different meanings of a word and how it is used in sentences.","Try to use two or three new words each week in your own writing or speaking."]}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-02-00-000-02-01';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Upper Intermediate', body = 'Study the [Academic Word List](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/wordlist/american_english/academic/), focusing on words you recognise but do not yet use.
Read longer articles from quality newspapers and magazines, and note useful phrases.
Pay attention to formal and informal choices (*buy* / *purchase*, *get* / *obtain*).
Learn common collocations and fixed phrases for academic writing (*play a key role*, *in terms of*).
When you write, replace general words (*good*, *big*, *thing*) with more precise ones.', fields_json = '{"level":"B2","bullets":["Study the [Academic Word List](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/wordlist/american_english/academic/), focusing on words you recognise but do not yet use.","Read longer articles from quality newspapers and magazines, and note useful phrases.","Pay attention to formal and informal choices (*buy* / *purchase*, *get* / *obtain*).","Learn common collocations and fixed phrases for academic writing (*play a key role*, *in terms of*).","When you write, replace general words (*good*, *big*, *thing*) with more precise ones."]}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-02-00-000-02-02';
UPDATE feedback_items SET heading = 'Advanced', body = 'Read widely across subjects: opinion pieces, reviews, academic articles and literature.
Study near-synonyms and the differences between them (*claim*, *assert*, *allege*).
Notice idioms, phrasal verbs and word connotations (positive, negative or neutral), and record them with examples.
Use a corpus or a collocation dictionary to check natural word combinations.
Practise paraphrasing short passages in your own words to use more of the vocabulary you know.', fields_json = '{"level":"C1","bullets":["Read widely across subjects: opinion pieces, reviews, academic articles and literature.","Study near-synonyms and the differences between them (*claim*, *assert*, *allege*).","Notice idioms, phrasal verbs and word connotations (positive, negative or neutral), and record them with examples.","Use a corpus or a collocation dictionary to check natural word combinations.","Practise paraphrasing short passages in your own words to use more of the vocabulary you know."]}' WHERE feedback_code = '01-02-00-000-02-03';
